{
  "basis": "the size written in the \\work{} heading, in Edward Hopper's hand",
  "note": "The size is the one figure on these leaves the artist wrote himself — the inside cover of Book I says the drawings and their lettering are his and everything else is hers — and it is the only quantity in the archive that measures an object rather than reporting an event. Read from the heading only: sizes mentioned in the prose are remarks about a size rather than statements of one.",
  "normalisation": "Nothing is rounded and nothing is reordered. 23 1/2 × 28 and 23 1/2 × 28 3/4 are two formats because they are two things she wrote, and the first figure stays the first figure — she writes height then width, so 35 × 60 is a very wide canvas and not a tall one. The fractions are parsed to numbers so the shapes can be plotted; the label keeps the figures as written.",
